Starting with the smallest blackbox test first, per the plan: `test_delete_black_node_one_red_child`. This is the one that directly proves the fixup isn't a no-op — root `b`(B) with red left child `a`; deleting `b` promotes `a`, and only the final `if (x != NULL) x->color = RB_BLACK;` line makes it pass `rb_validate`'s "root must be black" check.

I'd also update the stale comment block at lines 246–251, which still says fixup is "a no-op stub" and lists these cases as "deferred" — that's no longer true.

Proposed diff for `tests/test_rbtree.c`:

```c
/* Delete slice 1 covered only the BST-splice orchestration. delete_fixup is
 * now implemented (see src/rbtree.c); the cases below exercise real
 * rebalancing on top of that, in addition to the earlier fixup-free cases. */
```
(replacing lines 246–251)

and a new test, inserted after `test_delete_two_children_deep_successor_fixup_free` / before `main`:

```c
static void test_delete_black_node_one_red_child(void) {
    rbtree_t *t = create_test_tree(NULL);
    int values[2];
    const char *keys[] = {"b", "a"};
    for (size_t i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        values[i] = (int)i;
        assert(rb_insert(t, keys[i], &values[i]) == 0);
    }
    /* b(B) root, a(R) left leaf. Deleting "b" (black, one red child)
     * promotes "a" into the root slot -- rb_validate's "root must be
     * black" check fails here unless delete_fixup actually recolors it. */
    assert(rb_validate(t) == 0);
    assert(rb_delete(t, "b") == 0);
    assert(rb_size(t) == 1);
    assert(rb_find(t, "b") == NULL);
    assert(rb_find(t, "a") == &values[1]);
    assert(rb_validate(t) == 0);
    rb_destroy(t);
}
```

plus adding `test_delete_black_node_one_red_child();` to `main()` right after `test_delete_two_children_deep_successor_fixup_free();`.

